      "White opens with the king's pawn, taking the centre and freeing the queen and f1-bishop. You will fight for d4 from the side with your c-pawn.",
      "The Sicilian. You control d4 from the wing and create an unbalanced position from move one. If White opens the centre with d4, you trade your c-pawn for White's d-pawn and keep an extra central pawn for the middlegame.",
      "White develops and prepares d4 to open the position. This is the most common move; anti-Sicilians such as 2.c3, 2.Nc3 or 3.Bb5+ are the other systems you need to know.",
      "You control e5, open the c8-bishop and keep the move order that leads to the Najdorf. It also prepares ...Nf6 without allowing e5 to kick the knight, since the d6 pawn covers e5.",
      "White opens the centre: the Open Sicilian. White gets a lead in development and space; you get the half-open c-file and the extra centre pawn once you take.",
      "Take. You trade a wing pawn (c5) for a centre pawn (d4), leaving two central pawns (d6 and e7) against White's one (e4). In the long run that extra centre pawn is one of your main trumps.",
      "White recaptures with the knight, centralising it. Now you need to develop with tempo before White builds up with Nc3, Be3 and either kingside castling or a queenside castle and pawn storm.",
      "Develop with an attack on e4. White must defend it, usually with Nc3, which blocks the c-pawn and rules out the c4 Maroczy Bind set-up.",
      "White defends e4 with the knight. This is the standard Open Sicilian position, and your fifth move decides which Sicilian you play: ...a6 for the Najdorf, ...g6 for the Dragon, ...e6 for the Scheveningen or ...Nc6 for the Classical.",
