      "You strike at e4 and White must decide: defend it with 3.Nc3 or 3.Nd2, advance with 3.e5, or exchange with 3.exd5. Because of your c6-pawn, a capture on d5 is always met by a pawn recapture and your centre stays solid.",
      "White defends e4 with a developing move and keeps every option open. The knight on c3 blocks the c-pawn, so White gives up the c4 lever for quick piece play. 3.Nd2 usually reaches the same positions after 3...dxe4 4.Nxe4.",
      "You give up the centre point to free your game. Holding d5 with ...e6 would shut in your c8-bishop, so you trade the d-pawn for the e-pawn and plan to hit White's knight when it lands on e4.",
      "White recaptures and the knight stands in the centre, where it is also a target. White keeps a small space edge thanks to the d4-pawn against your c6-pawn, which is why the Caro-Kann is solid but a little passive.",
      "The Classical move. The bishop comes outside the pawn chain with a hit on the e4-knight. White's normal reply is 5.Ng3, chasing the bishop at once.",
      "This defends the knight, but the bishop now stands between White's queen and the d4-pawn. The pawn is suddenly unprotected, and your queen on d8 sees it straight down the open d-file.",
      "Win the pawn. It is not poisoned: White has no quick way to trap your queen, and the e4-knight is still attacked by your bishop.",
      "White develops and hits your queen, hoping a lead in development will make up for the pawn. Watch out for Nd6+ and other jumps by the e4-knight.",
      "Bring the queen home. Greedy tries like ...Qb6 or ...Qd5 hand White more time. On d8 the queen is safe and still covers d6.",
      "The queen protects the e4-knight and lines up on the e-file. White wants to castle and use the open lines, so trade pieces and develop quickly.",
      "Block the e-file and open the f8-bishop. Your king will be safe after castling, and with an extra pawn, safety is all you need.",
      "White completes kingside development. The pieces look active, but there are no real targets in your camp.",
      "Give up the bishop for the knight. It removes the e4-knight, which was eyeing d6 and f6, and every trade favours the side with the extra pawn.",
      "White has the bishop pair as some compensation. The e4-bishop eyes your queenside, so develop with tempo against it.",
      "The knight hits the bishop and covers h7 and d5. Each developing move with tempo shrinks White's compensation.",
      "White keeps the bishop on the b1-h7 diagonal. Bf3 is the other choice.",
      "Develop the last minor piece and get ready for ...Be7 and ...O-O. You are a pawn up with a solid position. The lesson: when a piece blocks the queen's defence of a pawn, count again who guards it.",
